<mujoco model="tong">
  <!-- A spring-loaded tong: two arms on one hinge, held open by a torsion
       spring, closing only while something pushes them shut.

       THE HINGE ZERO IS THE OPEN POSITION, and that is a portability
       requirement rather than a style choice. MuJoCo and Newton let a
       joint spring rest at a stated angle (`springref`), but Genesis has
       no such concept — its passive joint force is `-stiffness * q`
       (`genesis/engine/solvers/rigid/abd/forward_dynamics.py:985`), so
       its spring always rests at zero. Defining "fully open" as q = 0
       makes all three agree exactly, with no springref anywhere. Author
       this the other way round and Genesis alone would quietly hold the
       tong at a different opening.

       The travel runs from -0.15 rad to 0.321, and the open END of that
       is NOT where the tong sits. A spring tong's open position is set by
       its spring; the only place the arms physically stop each other is
       closed, where the pads meet. Giving the spring's rest position a
       hard limit in the same place puts two constraints in permanent
       disagreement over one point — the spring pushing into the stop and
       the stop pushing back, every step, forever. MuJoCo and Genesis
       absorb that; mujoco_warp turns a 0.24 m/s landing into an 11.7 m/s
       launch and NaN within a dozen steps, measured. The extra travel is
       headroom the spring never uses.

       q = 0.000 rad  fully open, 35 mm between the pads
       q = 0.094 rad  closed onto a 25 mm cube
       q = 0.321 rad  pads meet

       Both arms carry an explicit <inertial>: left to a solver's own
       geom-derived estimate, the three would not necessarily agree, and
       an inertia mismatch shows up as a different swing rate rather than
       as an error. The mass is stated once here and every backend reads
       it. -->
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <default>
    <default class="tong">
      <geom type="box" rgba="0.72 0.74 0.78 1" friction="0.9 0.005 0.0001"
            contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="3"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Lower arm, and the body the tong is carried by. Runs along +x
         from the pivot at the origin; its top face is the z = 0 plane. -->
    <body name="tong_base" pos="0 0 0" childclass="tong">
      <freejoint name="tong_free"/>
      <inertial pos="0.06 0 0" mass="0.03" diaginertia="2.34e-6 3.825e-5 3.609e-5"/>
      <geom name="tong_base_arm" size="0.06 0.003 0.015" pos="0.06 0.003 0"/>
      <geom name="tong_base_pad" size="0.008 0.003 0.015" pos="0.112 -0.003 0"/>
      <!-- Where the robot is meant to hold the tool: midway between the
           two arms, 60 mm out from the pivot. A task aiming a gripper
           at the tong needs a point to aim AT, and its frame origin is
           the wrong one twice over - the origin is the pivot, where the
           arms already touch so closing on it does nothing, and lying on
           a bench the pivot is half an arm's width off the surface, so a
           gripper sent there goes through the bench.

           Out at 60 mm the arms are 25 mm apart, well inside the 76 mm
           the YAM gripper opens to, and closing across them is the same
           motion that works the tool - which is what the later stages
           need and what a grasp at the pivot could never become. -->
      <site name="grip_site" pos="0.06 -0.0125 0" size="0.004" rgba="1 0.5 0 1"/>

      <!-- Upper arm. The body frame is pre-rotated by the open angle
           (0.428 rad about -y) so that inside it the arm runs along +x
           exactly like the lower one: the inertia is then diagonal in
           the body frame and identical to the lower arm's, which is one
           less thing that can differ between backends. Turning the joint
           positive rotates the arm back down, i.e. CLOSES it. -->
      <body name="tong_jaw" pos="0 0 0" quat="0.977194 0 0 -0.212371">
        <joint name="tong_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" range="-0.15 0.321"
               stiffness="0.5" damping="0.008"/>
        <inertial pos="0.06 0 0" mass="0.03" diaginertia="2.34e-6 3.825e-5 3.609e-5"/>
        <geom name="tong_jaw_arm" size="0.06 0.003 0.015" pos="0.06 -0.003 0"/>
        <geom name="tong_jaw_pad" size="0.008 0.003 0.015" pos="0.112 0.003 0"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- The two arms overlap at their shared pivot, as the links of any
       hinge do. MuJoCo filters that pair for being parent and child, so
       a standalone tong behaves; loaded as one entity among several in a
       scene it does not, and the pair starts colliding. The contact then
       pushes the arms APART, which drives the hinge past its own open
       stop and holds it there: measured at -0.12 rad on mjlab and
       Newton against +0.033 on Genesis, whose scene disables
       self-collision and so never saw it.

       Excluded explicitly, so the answer depends neither on a backend's
       reimplementation of the parent rule nor on whether whoever builds
       the scene remembered to turn self-collision off. -->
  <contact>
    <exclude body1="tong_base" body2="tong_jaw"/>
  </contact>
</mujoco>
